**EXERCISE 4.10.** Given a domain $D$ and an element $a \in |D|$, construct a domain $D_a$ where

$$
|D_a| = \{x \in |D| \mid x \sqsubseteq a\}.
$$

Show that if $f : D \to D$ is approximable, then $f$ can be restricted to an approximable map $f' : D_{\mathrm{fix}(f)} \to D_{\mathrm{fix}(f)}$ where $f'(x) = f(x)$ for all $x \in |D_{\mathrm{fix}(f)}|$. How many fixed points does $f'$ have in $|D_{\mathrm{fix}(f)}|$?

<!-- page 68 -->

The relative domain, induced endomap, and inclusion are with the
[domain above an element](1980_relSystem.md) peer.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`example_410`).** A fixed point below the least fixed-point element equals that least fixed point, and the relative endomap on the least fixed-point seed has a unique fixed point, namely the restricted least fixed-point element.

*Proof.* Invoke uniqueness of fixed points below the least fixed-point element and uniqueness of relative fixed points on that seed. ∎
